st, so it falls short by about 99 units. That gives roughly 8,830 before storage and 8,530 after.\n- Candidate 3 orders about 15% above forecast. It falls short by about 29 units and writes off about 150. That gives roughly 9,410 before storage and 8,980 after.\n- Candidate 5 orders about 30% above forecast. It falls short by only about 6 units but writes off about 300, and storage costs more. That gives roughly 8,265 after storage.\n\n**Product 1.** Candidate 0 orders about 0.9 of forecast, candidate 3 about 1.0 and candidate 5 about 1.1. Product 1 earns little per unit, so here candidate 0 is roughly 50–100 ahead of candidate 3. That doesn't make up the roughly 450 it loses on product 0.\n\n**Against the daily plans.** Candidate 2 is the strongest of them, with about the same stock levels as candidate 3. It still pays about 660 more in order fees and saves only about 520 in storage, so it ends up about 150–200 behind.\n\n**When demand differs from forecast.** Candidate 3 holds up best. Shortages cost much more than leftovers, which can still be sold from the next batch for up to two more days. So its 15% buffer protects against high-demand days without the heavy write-offs of candidate 5."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4972, "cache_read_input_tokens": 2269, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4972}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-25T18:51:38.882Z", "request_id": "req_011CgPTTKd7QqQXNvUJ9qZcf"}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 1}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 2, "content_block": {"type": "tool_use", 